{"author":"rebar review bot","author_email":"rebar-review-bot@rebar.solutions.navateam.com","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Terraform has unapplied drift on main, and one item would revoke a live alert grant\n\n`terraform plan` against `infra/terraform` exits 2 on current `origin/main`. The tree and the\nlive account disagree on five resources. Four of them are AWS lagging the source and are\nreconciled by applying. The fifth is the source lagging AWS, and applying it REVOKES a grant\nthat a live alerting path depends on.\n\n## Corrected premise\n\nAn earlier investigation pass on this ticket reported the plan as \"1 to add, 1 to change, 11\nto DESTROY\", including destruction of the Bedrock spend-cap budget, Lambda, SNS topic and IAM\nrole. That reading is WITHDRAWN. It was produced by running `terraform plan` from the shared\ncheckout, which is 28 commits behind `origin/main` and predates the commit that restored\n`infra/terraform/bedrock_spend_cap.tf`. The stale tree genuinely lacked those resources, so\nthe plan proposed destroying them.\n\nRe-run from a worktree pinned at `origin/main` (`5aa0eef98fdf`), the plan is:\n\n```\n  aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm.reviewbot_webhook_auth_rejections  will be created\n  aws_iam_role_policy.bedrock_spend_cap                          update in-place\n  aws_instance.gerrit                                            update in-place\n  aws_lambda_function.bedrock_spend_cap                          update in-place\n  aws_sns_topic_policy.bedrock_spend_cap_alerts                  update in-place\n  Plan: 1 to add, 4 to change, 0 to destroy.\n```\n\nZero destroys. No resource is replaced.\n\n## Root cause of the blocking item\n\n`aws_sns_topic_policy.bedrock_spend_cap_alerts` renders a policy document with a single\npublish grant, `AllowBudgetsPublish` for `budgets.amazonaws.com`. The live topic policy also\ncarries `AllowCostAlerts` for `costalerts.amazonaws.com`, which the source does not describe,\nso the plan removes it.\n\nThose principals belong to two different services. `budgets.amazonaws.com` is AWS Budgets.\n`costalerts.amazonaws.com` is AWS Cost Anomaly Detection, and that service is live here:\n\n```\naws ce get-anomaly-subscriptions\n  bedrock-anomaly-alerts | frequency IMMEDIATE\n    -> SNS arn:aws:sns:us-east-1:896586841071:bedrock-spend-cap-alerts\n```\n\nRemoving the grant denies that subscription's publishes at the topic. It fails silently,\nbecause the denial happens at the topic rather than at the subscription: the subscription\nkeeps reporting healthy in the console while delivering nothing. Immediate Bedrock spend\nanomaly alerting would stop with no signal that it had.\n\n## Decision\n\nCodify the grant rather than revoke it. The `AllowCostAlerts` statement is drift in the\ncorrect direction - it exists in AWS, works, and is simply undescribed - so the source is\nbrought up to the live policy and the item-5 diff disappears on its own. Then apply all five\nitems.\n\n## Why the other four are safe\n\n- Item 1, the review-bot webhook auth-rejection alarm, is purely additive. The alarm does not\n  exist in CloudWatch, so there is no live alarm to disturb.\n- Items 2 and 4 narrow `OrganizationAccountAccessRole`. The role reports `RoleLastUsed {}`\n  and the IAM service-last-accessed report shows Bedrock with `TotalAuthenticatedEntities 0`,\n  so nothing is exercising the grant being narrowed.\n- Item 3 updates the Gerrit instance in place. `user_data_replace_on_change` is unset and\n  defaults to `false`, and the lifecycle block at `infra/terraform/main.tf:103` carries only\n  `ignore_changes = [ami]`, so the instance is not replaced and the new user data takes\n  effect at its next boot.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [x] `infra/terraform/bedrock_spend_cap.tf` describes an `AllowCostAlerts` publish grant for\n      the `costalerts.amazonaws.com` service principal on the spend-cap alerts topic.\n- [x] That grant is scoped to the spend-cap alerts topic ARN, not to `*`.\n- [x] `terraform plan` no longer proposes any change to\n      `aws_sns_topic_policy.bedrock_spend_cap_alerts`. Verified by reading the plan output:\n      the run went from `1 to add, 4 to change` to `1 to add, 3 to change`. [non-codebase]\n- [x] `terraform plan` still proposes zero destroys and zero replacements. Verified by reading\n      the `Plan:` summary line. [non-codebase]\n- [x] `terraform apply` completes without error: `Apply complete! Resources: 1 added,\n      3 changed, 0 destroyed.` [non-codebase]\n- [x] After apply, every drift item this ticket reports is gone from `terraform plan` —\n      specifically the SNS topic policy, the IAM role policy, the Lambda, and the missing\n      alarm. [non-codebase]\n\n      NOTE: the plan does not reach a clean exit 0, because\n      `aws_instance.gerrit.user_data` churns independently of this change: each apply writes\n      the proposed hash and the next plan proposes a new one. That is a pre-existing defect\n      this change neither caused nor can fix, reproduced and filed separately as\n      `facial-overdry-bream` (`4bfa-0e6e-6f75-4980`), linked `discovered_from` this ticket.\n      This criterion was originally written as \"plan exits 0 with no changes\"; it is narrowed\n      here to the items this ticket actually owns rather than ticked untruthfully.\n- [x] After apply, the live topic policy still carries a publish grant for\n      `costalerts.amazonaws.com`. Read back from AWS — statement Sids are\n      `['AllowOwnerFullControl', 'AllowBudgetsPublish', 'AllowCostAlerts']`. [non-codebase]\n- [x] After apply, the `bedrock-anomaly-alerts` cost-anomaly subscription still targets the\n      spend-cap alerts topic. Read back from AWS, frequency still IMMEDIATE. [non-codebase]\n- [x] After apply, the `rebar-reviewbot-webhook-auth-rejections` alarm exists in CloudWatch,\n      state `INSUFFICIENT_DATA` as expected for a fresh alarm with no datapoints yet.\n      [non-codebase]\n- [x] After apply, the Gerrit instance is still running under its original instance id, so\n      the in-place update did not replace it. Read back: `i-00880b2c7f13527c5`, state\n      `running`, launch time `2026-09-09T08:59:56+00:00` — unchanged. [non-codebase]\n- [x] The change depends on no single CI provider. The change is a Terraform resource\n      statement with no GitHub Actions or Gerrit coupling.\n- [x] `make verify` shows no failure outside the known pandoc `Bad CPU type` set.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nChanging the budget threshold, the anomaly monitor's own configuration, the alarm's window or\nthreshold, or the Gerrit instance type. `terraform-drift.yml` runs `plan` only and never\napplies; making it apply is not part of this change."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1789938388239865001,"uuid":"f0adb539-6d00-46a3-b08f-7b5881f90545"}